## Summary

Most FGD respondents indicated that deforestation, charcoal production and fire frequency were decreasing, wildlife populations were increasing and natural resource management was improving within CFs. Most FGD groups indicated that both deforestation and charcoal production were also decreasing outside their CF, suggesting there is no leakage or a positive leakage effect. Community female FGDs tended to have a more positive view of the impact of CFM compared to the other groups, which is noteworthy given it is primarily the women who harvest forest products, but who often do not have voice in traditional natural resource management. However, it is also important to note that a small number of FGD groups and individual interviewees suggested the situation was worsening in their CFs. The Governance Index was a significant predictor of performance, according to the perceptions expressed by the FGDs. Not many CFMGs were practising restoration or establishing plantations. Existing CFs should be expanded and new ones designed so that they incorporate degraded areas that can become the focus of restoration and plantation activities and contribute to national restoration and plantation targets.

::: {.callout-note icon="false"}
## Key conclusions

CFM in Zambia is demonstrably improving SFM across the large majority of surveyed communities, with positive spillover effects in the surrounding landscape and encouraging evidence of women's engagement in forest governance. The opportunity to build on these gains through a more systematic approach to restoration and plantation is substantial and should not be deferred.
:::

## Introduction

In this chapter, we consider the effect of community forestry on indicators of SFM. The information analysed is based entirely only on the responses of interviewees to our survey tool. In the next chapter we will consider the effects of CF on SMF as measured through remote sensing derived metrics.

### Deforestation

We asked interviewees whether CFM has reduced deforestation inside their CF and whether or not deforestation has increased outside their CF.

#### Deforestation inside the community forest


::: {.cell}
::: {.cell-output-display}
![ 'Has CFM reduced deforestation inside the community forest?' The length of each bar segment represents the proportion of FGD groups in that category. The numbers superimposed on the bars represent the actual number of groups in each category.](CFMG-SFM_files/figure-html/fig-reduced_forest_clearing-1.png){#fig-reduced_forest_clearing width=672}
:::
:::


::: return-to-summary
[← Return to Summary](Summary.html#sustainable-forest-management)
:::

A majority of groups reported either a moderate or significant reduction in deforestation inside the CF (@fig-reduced_forest_clearing). Moreover, these answers were consistent across the FGD groups. There were no significant differences in response among FGD types (@tbl-M1_deforestation_fgd). However, the quality of governance, as measured by the governance index we generated in the previous chapter, was a highly significant predictor of the perceived decrease in deforestation within the CF (@tbl-M1_deforestation_fgd).




::: {#tbl-M1_deforestation_fgd .cell tbl-cap='Results of the model examining the effect of FGD type and governance index on perceived reduction in deforestation inside the CF.'}
::: {.cell-output-display}

